To handle the `InputMismatchException` in this program, we can add a try-catch block around the code that reads the input from the user. Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int num1 = 0, num2 = 0;
        boolean validInput = false;

        while (!validInput) {
            try {
                System.out.print("Enter first number: ");
                num1 = scanner.nextInt();
                System.out.print("Enter second number: ");
                num2 = scanner.nextInt();
                validInput = true;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter integers only.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining characters on the line
            }
        }
        // Perform calculations with num1 and num2 here
    }
}
```

In this modified version of the program, we declare two integer variables `num1` and `num2`, as well as a boolean flag `validInput`. Inside a `while` loop, we repeatedly prompt the user to enter two numbers until they provide valid input (i.e., integers). If the user enters non-integer values, the `InputMismatchException` is caught and the error message is displayed, followed by a call to `scanner.nextLine()` to consume any remaining characters on the current line (otherwise, they would be interpreted as part of the next input). Once valid input has been provided, we break out of the loop and proceed with our calculations.

This approach ensures that the program does not crash due to invalid input, provides clear feedback to the user, and allows us to maintain a cleaner and more readable main method.

